{"id":"ea28f139-c5bc-46ea-834c-e29be4816011","object":"chat.completion","created":1784821367,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"from collections import deque\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be a dict\\\")\\n    payload = case.get(\\\"payload\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(payload, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"missing payload\\\")\\n    nodes = payload.get(\\\"nodes\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or len(nodes) == 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"nodes missing or empty\\\")\\n    threshold = payload.get(\\\"utility_threshold\\\")\\n    if threshold is None:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"utility_threshold missing\\\")\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if not isinstance(n, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"node not dict\\\")\\n        nid = n.get(\\\"id\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(nid, str) or not nid:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"invalid node id\\\")\\n        parent = n.get(\\\"parent\\\")\\n        if parent is not None and not isinstance(parent, str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"invalid parent\\\")\\n        if \\\"depth\\\" not in n or not isinstance(n[\\\"depth\\\"], int):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"missing depth\\\")\\n        if \\\"removable\\\" not in n or not isinstance(n[\\\"removable\\\"], bool):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"missing removable\\\")\\n        if \\\"utility\\\" not in n or not isinstance(n[\\\"utility\\\"], (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"missing utility\\\")\\n        if nid in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"duplicate id\\\")\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    root_ids = [nid for nid, n in node_map.items() if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is None]\\n    if len(root_ids) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"must have exactly one root\\\")\\n    root_id = root_ids[0]\\n    all_ids = list(node_map.keys())\\n    all_ids.sort(key=lambda nid: (-node_map[nid][\\\"depth\\\"], nid))\\n    queue = deque(all_ids)\\n    removed = set()\\n    visit_order = []\\n    while queue:\\n        nid = queue.popleft()\\n        visit_order.append(nid)\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        node = node_map[nid]\\n        if node[\\\"removable\\\"] and node[\\\"utility\\\"] <= threshold:\\n            child_list = children.get(nid, [])\\n            if all(c in removed for c in child_list):\\n                removed.add(nid)\\n                parent = node[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n                if parent is not None:\\n                    if parent not in removed:\\n                        queue.append(parent)\\n    retained_ids = [nid for nid in all_ids if nid not in removed]\\n    return {\\\"retained_ids\\\": retained_ids, \\\"visit_order\\\": visit_order}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a Python function `solve(case)` that implements the described behavior. The task: \"parse_tree_reduction_queue\" with bounded objective: maintain queue of nodes in descending depth and stable node-ID order, skip removed nodes, remove only removable leaf whose utility <= threshold, after removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration. Return retained IDs and complete visit order.\n\nFrom description: \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" So output should have two keys: \"retained_ids\" (list of node IDs that are not removed) and \"visit_order\" (list of node IDs in the order they were processed). Actually \"complete visit order\" likely means the order in which nodes were visited/considered (including those not removed). Need to carefully read.\n\nAlso \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\" So we start with all nodes sorted by depth descending, then by node ID (presumably string order). But note \"stable node-ID order\" might mean alphabetical or as given? IDs are like \"root\", \"n1\", etc. Typically string sort.\n\nThe process: repeatedly take first node from queue. If the node is already removed (i.e., it was removed earlier), skip. Else if it is a removable leaf and its utility <= threshold, then remove it (mark as removed), and enqueue its parent (if parent exists and is not removed) for reconsideration. Otherwise, do nothing (maybe record it as visited). Continue until queue empty. Return final set of retained IDs (all nodes not removed) and visit order (sequence of node IDs as they are dequeued, including skipped ones? Or only those that were processed? \"complete visit order\" likely the order of dequeuing, including skipped nodes, since that's the queue processing order. But need to check: \"visit order\" might refer to the order nodes are considered for removal. In typical BFS/queue problems, visit order is the order they are popped. The description: \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" I think it's the order of dequeuing.\n\nAlso note: \"Skip nodes already removed.\" So if a node is popped and it's already removed (due to being parent of a removed child? Actually parents are not removed unless they were removed themselves. But if node is removed as part of removal, it won't be in the queue again because we only enqueue parents that are retained. But could there be duplicates? The queue might contain multiple entries for same node if enqueued multiple times. When we pop, we should skip if already removed. So we need a set of removed IDs.\n\nImplementation steps:\n- Parse case payload: nodes list, utility_threshold.\n- Validate required fields: each node has id, parent (null for root), symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also ensure root has parent null, etc. If invalid, raise ValueError.\n- Build mapping from id to node dict.\n- Identify root (parent null).\n- Initialize list of node ids sorted: primary key depth descending, secondary key node id (string). Since we need stable, we can sort by (-depth, id). Use Python's sort with key lambda.\n- Initialize queue = list of these ids (order sorted).\n- Initialize removed = set().\n- Initialize visit_order = [].\n- Process while queue:\n    - pop first element (pop(0) but O(n^2) if many nodes; for small cases it's fine but we can use collections.deque for efficiency; but standard library allowed. Use deque for O(1) popleft.)\n    - id = queue.popleft()\n    - visit_order.append(id)\n    - if id in removed: continue\n    - node = nodes_dict[id]\n    - if node[\"removable\"] and node[\"utility\"] <= utility_threshold and is_leaf(node, nodes_dict, removed): # leaf means no children that are not removed? Actually after removal, children are gone. But leaf means it has no children currently in the tree (i.e., not removed). Need to check children: For a node to be a leaf, it should have no children that are not removed. We can precompute children map.\n        - remove it: removed.add(id)\n        - parent_id = node[\"parent\"]\n        - if parent_id is not None: enqueue parent_id (add to queue right side) - but careful: we only enqueue if parent is not removed. Also we might want to enqueue only once? But the description says \"enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" So we add to queue. If parent already in queue, it's fine to have duplicates; we skip when popped but it's okay.\n    - else: do nothing.\n- After loop, retained_ids = [id for id in all_ids if id not in removed] sorted in stable order? The description doesn't specify order; likely sorted by depth descending then id? But \"return retained IDs\" might be any order, perhaps the original order? Not specified. Might be fine to just list all node IDs not removed, maybe sorted as per initial order (desc depth, then id). But to be safe, we can return them in the same order as the initial sorting (desc depth, id). Since the initial queue is sorted that way, we can produce that sorted list of retained IDs.\n- Return dict with keys \"retained_ids\" and \"visit_order\".\n\nEdge cases: root is not removable (removable false). But if root becomes leaf? Not possible because it's not removable. So fine.\n\nWe need to implement is_leaf: a node is leaf if it has no children in the current tree (i.e., no child that is not removed). We can precompute children mapping: dict child list. Then when checking, we can check if all children are in removed set. But note: if a child is removed, it's no longer a child, so leaf condition: no child that is not removed. So we can iterate over child list and see if any not in removed. If none, it's a leaf.\n\nAlso note: when we remove a node, we don't remove its children from the tree? But they are already removed (they must have been removed earlier or never existed). Actually we remove leaf only. So children are not affected because leaf has no children. However, after removing a node, its parent may become leaf. That's why we enqueue parent.\n\nAll good.\n\nImportant: \"Use only quantities present in the case payload.\" So we shouldn't use external info.\n\nTesting with given fixtures: case 000: nodes with utilities, threshold 0.55. Let's simulate manually partly.\n\nBut given the strict response format, we must output one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value string of Python source. Must escape newlines, quotes, backslashes properly.\n\nWe need to ensure the code is correct and handles all validations.\n\nLet's draft code:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\nimport json\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate case structure\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be a dict\")\n    payload = case.get(\"payload\")\n    if not isinstance(payload, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"missing payload\")\n    nodes = payload.get(\"nodes\")\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or len(nodes) == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"nodes missing or empty\")\n    threshold = payload.get(\"utility_threshold\")\n    if threshold is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"utility_threshold missing\")\n    # Validate each node\n    node_map = {}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if not isinstance(n, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"node not dict\")\n        nid = n.get(\"id\")\n        if not isinstance(nid, str) or not nid:\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid node id\")\n        parent = n.get(\"parent\")\n        if parent is not None and not isinstance(parent, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid parent\")\n        if \"depth\" not in n or not isinstance(n[\"depth\"], int):\n            raise ValueError(\"missing depth\")\n        if \"removable\" not in n or not isinstance(n[\"removable\"], bool):\n            raise ValueError(\"missing removable\")\n        if \"utility\" not in n or not isinstance(n[\"utility\"], (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"missing utility\")\n        if nid in node_map:\n            raise ValueError(\"duplicate id\")\n        node_map[nid] = n\n        children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n    # Ensure root with parent None\n    root_ids = [nid for nid, n in node_map.items() if n[\"parent\"] is None]\n    if len(root_ids) != 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"must have exactly one root\")\n    root_id = root_ids[0]\n    # Sort all node IDs by descending depth, then stable node ID (string)\n    all_ids = list(node_map.keys())\n    all_ids.sort(key=lambda nid: (-node_map[nid][\"depth\"], nid))\n    # Initialize queue\n    queue = deque(all_ids)\n    removed = set()\n    visit_order = []\n    while queue:\n        nid = queue.popleft()\n        visit_order.append(nid)\n        if nid in removed:\n            continue\n        node = node_map[nid]\n        if node[\"removable\"] and node[\"utility\"] <= threshold:\n            # Check if leaf: no child that is not removed\n            child_list = children.get(nid, [])\n            if all(c in removed for c in child_list):\n                # Remove this node\n                removed.add(nid)\n                parent = node[\"parent\"]\n                if parent is not None:\n                    if parent not in removed:\n                        queue.append(parent)\n                # Note: we do not remove children; they are already absent\n        # else do nothing\n    # Build retained_ids: all ids not removed, sorted as initial order (desc depth, stable id)\n    retained_ids = [nid for nid in all_ids if nid not in removed]\n    return {\"retained_ids\": retained_ids, \"visit_order\": visit_order}\n```\n\nBut we need to ensure that the output is JSON-serializable. Python lists of strings are fine.\n\nCheck: The case payload is a dict with \"payload\" key, etc. The function receives the entire case dict. So we access case[\"payload\"].\n\nValidation: we need to raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid. The given fixtures are valid but we must handle general.\n\nEdge: root may be removable? Possibly but in fixtures root removable false. If root is removable and becomes leaf? But we don't remove root because maybe threshold? But description says \"remove only a removable leaf\". If root is leaf (no children) and removable and utility <= threshold, then we would remove root. That's allowed. But then parent is None, so we don't enqueue anything. That's fine.\n\nAlso note: after removing a node, we enqueue its parent. That parent might already be in queue (maybe later). That's okay.\n\nNow ensure visit_order: includes all nodes popped, even if skipped (removed already) or not removed. That matches \"complete visit order\".\n\nOne subtlety: When we remove a leaf, we remove it. But what about its children? Leaf has no children. So fine.\n\nBut consider: after removal, a parent that previously had multiple children may become leaf if all its children are removed. When we enqueue that parent, later when popped, it might satisfy leaf condition and get removed. So algorithm works.\n\nNow check the initial queue order: sorted by depth descending, then node ID stable. In Python, string comparison is lexicographic, but IDs are like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. That's fine.\n\nPotential issue: The fixture output is not given, so we cannot verify exact result. But we trust the process.\n\nNow we need to output JSON with escaped source. Let's produce.\n\nWe'll write the source as a string with proper escaping. Since we are generating JSON in response, we need to embed the Python code as a string value for key \"implementation\". That string must be valid JSON string, so we need to escape backslashes, quotes, newlines. We can manually escape or use json.dumps in thought. I'll write the code carefully.\n\nWe'll produce the final JSON object:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"from collections import deque\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be a dict\\\")\\n    payload = case.get(\\\"payload\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(payload, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"missing payload\\\")\\n    nodes = payload.get(\\\"nodes\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or len(nodes) == 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"nodes missing or empty\\\")\\n    threshold = payload.get(\\\"utility_threshold\\\")\\n    if threshold is None:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"utility_threshold missing\\\")\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if not isinstance(n, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"node not dict\\\")\\n        nid = n.get(\\\"id\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(nid, str) or not nid:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"invalid node id\\\")\\n        parent = n.get(\\\"parent\\\")\\n        if parent is not None and not isinstance(parent, str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"invalid parent\\\")\\n        if \\\"depth\\\" not in n or not isinstance(n[\\\"depth\\\"], int):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"missing depth\\\")\\n        if \\\"removable\\\" not in n or not isinstance(n[\\\"removable\\\"], bool):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"missing removable\\\")\\n        if \\\"utility\\\" not in n or not isinstance(n[\\\"utility\\\"], (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"missing utility\\\")\\n        if nid in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"duplicate id\\\")\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    root_ids = [nid for nid, n in node_map.items() if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is None]\\n    if len(root_ids) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"must have exactly one root\\\")\\n    root_id = root_ids[0]\\n    all_ids = list(node_map.keys())\\n    all_ids.sort(key=lambda nid: (-node_map[nid][\\\"depth\\\"], nid))\\n    queue = deque(all_ids)\\n    removed = set()\\n    visit_order = []\\n    while queue:\\n        nid = queue.popleft()\\n        visit_order.append(nid)\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        node = node_map[nid]\\n        if node[\\\"removable\\\"] and node[\\\"utility\\\"] <= threshold:\\n            child_list = children.get(nid, [])\\n            if all(c in removed for c in child_list):\\n                removed.add(nid)\\n                parent = node[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n                if parent is not None:\\n                    if parent not in removed:\\n                        queue.append(parent)\\n    retained_ids = [nid for nid in all_ids if nid not in removed]\\n    return {\\\"retained_ids\\\": retained_ids, \\\"visit_order\\\": visit_order}\"\n\nBut we need to ensure that the string is properly escaped. In the final JSON, backslashes must be escaped. For example, newline \\n becomes \\\\n. Also double quotes inside string become \\\". We'll need to produce the escaped version. Let me write the code as a raw string and then manually escape. Alternatively, I can think of using Python's json.dumps on the code string, but since I'm generating JSON manually, I'll do careful escaping.\n\nI'll create the source code with indentation using spaces, and then replace: newlines w